Scott's Proposition 7.7 states that if \(D\) is effectively given then so is \(D^\S\), and that every combinator of Example 6.1 is computable; the opening claim that \(\lambda,!\)-expressions over effectively given types are a metalanguage for computable operators is not a statement in this closure. `theorem_77` is only the first half, and it carries the hypothesis, used already to build `Dsharp`, that every neighbourhood of \(D\) is nonempty: `dsharp_isEffectivelyGiven` takes `dsharpPresentation` as the witness. The enumeration `Vsharp` is Scott's indexing on the carrier `List Bool × α` of \(\Gamma=\{1,2\}^*0\Delta\): index \(0\) is the master `Gamma`, index \(2n+1\) is `embZero` of the \(n\)th neighbourhood, and index \(2n+2\) is `embPair` of the values at the two components of `Nat.unpair n`, with `Vsharp_mem` and `Vsharp_surj` giving \(D^\S=\{V_k\}\). Scott's writeup instead replaces the tokens by the residue partition \(\mathbf{N}=3\mathbf{N}\cup(3\mathbf{N}+1)\cup(3\mathbf{N}+2)\), sets \(V_0=\mathbf{N}\), \(V_{2n+1}=3X_n\), \(V_{2n+2}=(3V_{p(n)}+1)\cup(3V_{q(n)}+2)\), and requires \(p(n)<n\) and \(q(n)<n\); `unpair_fst_le` gives only a component at most \(n\), which still makes the recursion on the index \(2n+2\) decrease. He also assumes that token membership \(k\in X_n\) is recursive, whereas the check of 7.1(i)–(ii) uses the presentation deciders: `dsharp_decider_spec` inducts on the pair code, and `dsharpStep` returns, against a zero index, the pack of \(1-j\), consistency bit \(1\), and intersection index \(j\) (meet with the master, equality only at the master, by `Vsharp_eq_Gamma_iff`); on two odd indices, \(D\)'s equality and consistency bits and one more than twice \(D\)'s intersection index (`dsharpStep_ll`, `Vsharp_odd_inter_odd`); on mixed parity, the zero pack (`dsharpStep_ln`, `dsharpStep_nl`, empty by `Vsharp_odd_inter_even`); on two even indices, the products of the two children's memoized bits and the node index two more than twice the pair of their intersection indices (`dsharpStep_nn`, `Vsharp_even_inter_even`), the children being read from the course-of-values table `gOf`/`rtbl` by `listGet`. `dsharp_intI_correct` and `dsharp_interEq_iff` turn those bits into the intersection-equality decider, and `primrec_dsharpStep` with `primrec_gOf` makes both deciders primitive recursive. The gloss of `dsharpPresentation` says the tokens are the sharp neighbourhoods; the carrier is `List Bool × α`, and `Vsharp` enumerates its subsets. Of the Example 6.1 combinators, the closure proves only the sharp injection and \(\mathrm{proj}_0\). `inSharp_isComputable` decides \(X_n\,(\lambda x.\,x^\S)\,V_k\) by inclusion of the zero-embedding at the odd index \(2n+1\), which is the first displayed biconditional, and `inSharpMap_toElementMap` sends an element to its sharp image. `proj0_rel_Vsharp_iff` is the second: the relation holds if and only if \(k=0\) or \(m\) is even and nonzero and the neighbourhood at the left component of \(m/2-1\), that is \(V_{p(n)}\) when \(m=2n+2\), is contained in \(V_k\); `proj0_isComputable` makes that predicate decidable, hence recursively enumerable, and `proj0_toElementMap_pairSharp` gives \(\mathrm{proj}_0\langle x,y\rangle=x\). The pairing \(\lambda x,y.\,\langle x,y\rangle\), \(\mathrm{proj}_1\), \(\mathrm{out}\), and \(\mathrm{atom}\) are not proved computable. **Theorem:** partial — effective givenness of \(D^\S\) is `theorem_77`, but only `inSharp` and `proj0` among the Example 6.1 combinators are shown computable. **Proof:** partial — the index recursion and the reduction of consistency, intersection, and equality to \(D\) or to strictly smaller indices are `dsharp_decider_spec`, while the mod-3 arithmetic and the remaining combinator cases are absent.
